1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,717
One hundred and twenty-one thousand

2
00:00:02,717 --> 00:00:04,500
dollars a year.

3
00:00:04,860 --> 00:00:06,913
That is roughly the income you now

4
00:00:06,913 --> 00:00:08,966
need to afford the payments on the

5
00:00:08,966 --> 00:00:11,392
typical American home.

6
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:12,987
Five years earlier,

7
00:00:12,987 --> 00:00:15,714
it was sixty-eight thousand seven hundred

8
00:00:15,714 --> 00:00:16,840
dollars.

9
00:00:16,840 --> 00:00:19,020
The houses did not get bigger.

10
00:00:19,020 --> 00:00:20,790
They did not get better.

11
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:23,121
The same home simply started to cost

12
00:00:23,121 --> 00:00:25,774
a whole extra salary.

13
00:00:26,017 --> 00:00:27,424
So how did a house,

14
00:00:27,424 --> 00:00:30,739
the most ordinary thing in American life,

15
00:00:30,739 --> 00:00:32,647
slip out of reach for so many

16
00:00:32,647 --> 00:00:33,724
people?

17
00:00:33,983 --> 00:00:35,850
By the end of this video,

18
00:00:35,850 --> 00:00:37,977
you will know the four forces behind

19
00:00:37,977 --> 00:00:38,773
it.

20
00:00:38,773 --> 00:00:39,445
The price,

21
00:00:39,445 --> 00:00:40,117
the loan,

22
00:00:40,117 --> 00:00:42,178
the homes that were never built,

23
00:00:42,178 --> 00:00:44,263
and the rules that decide what can

24
00:00:44,263 --> 00:00:45,790
be built at all.

25
00:00:51,450 --> 00:00:54,250
Start with a simple yardstick.

26
00:00:54,370 --> 00:00:56,617
Take the price of a typical existing

27
00:00:56,617 --> 00:00:58,456
home and divide it by the income

28
00:00:58,456 --> 00:01:00,690
of a typical household.

29
00:01:00,870 --> 00:01:03,463
Harvard's Joint Center for Housing Studies

30
00:01:03,463 --> 00:01:05,360
tracks exactly that.

31
00:01:05,750 --> 00:01:07,717
In the nineteen nineties,

32
00:01:07,717 --> 00:01:09,486
a home cost about three point two

33
00:01:09,486 --> 00:01:10,970
years of income.

34
00:01:11,310 --> 00:01:12,881
In twenty nineteen,

35
00:01:12,881 --> 00:01:14,620
about four point one.

36
00:01:14,680 --> 00:01:16,400
In twenty twenty-five,

37
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:18,119
four point seven times,

38
00:01:18,119 --> 00:01:20,362
just below the all-time high of five

39
00:01:20,362 --> 00:01:22,956
reached in twenty twenty-two.

40
00:01:23,217 --> 00:01:26,117
The biggest jump came fast.

41
00:01:26,187 --> 00:01:28,172
According to the same report,

42
00:01:28,172 --> 00:01:30,180
home prices rose fifty-four percent

43
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:32,787
nationwide after twenty twenty.

44
00:01:33,087 --> 00:01:34,759
Wages rose too,

45
00:01:34,759 --> 00:01:37,407
but nowhere near that fast.

46
00:01:37,650 --> 00:01:40,414
New homes tell the same story over

47
00:01:40,414 --> 00:01:42,120
a longer stretch.

48
00:01:42,300 --> 00:01:44,865
The Census Bureau's median price for a

49
00:01:44,865 --> 00:01:47,173
newly built house sold in the summer

50
00:01:47,173 --> 00:01:49,482
of two thousand was one hundred and

51
00:01:49,482 --> 00:01:51,277
sixty-eight thousand eight hundred

52
00:01:51,277 --> 00:01:52,390
dollars.

53
00:01:52,470 --> 00:01:54,732
In the summer of twenty twenty-six,

54
00:01:54,732 --> 00:01:56,807
it was three hundred and ninety-three

55
00:01:56,807 --> 00:01:58,860
thousand three hundred dollars.

56
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:01,006
These are dollars of the day,

57
00:02:01,006 --> 00:02:03,265
not adjusted for inflation,

58
00:02:03,265 --> 00:02:05,643
but the price more than doubled.

59
00:02:05,883 --> 00:02:08,203
Let me draw the yardstick.

60
00:02:08,203 --> 00:02:10,280
Here is one year of a typical

61
00:02:10,280 --> 00:02:11,641
household's income,

62
00:02:11,641 --> 00:02:12,703
one block.

63
00:02:12,883 --> 00:02:14,093
In the nineties,

64
00:02:14,093 --> 00:02:15,733
a home was a stack of about

65
00:02:15,733 --> 00:02:16,743
three blocks.

66
00:02:16,833 --> 00:02:18,571
By twenty nineteen,

67
00:02:18,571 --> 00:02:19,423
four.

68
00:02:19,553 --> 00:02:20,556
Today,

69
00:02:20,556 --> 00:02:22,193
almost five.

70
00:02:22,233 --> 00:02:24,983
Every extra block is a whole extra

71
00:02:24,983 --> 00:02:25,877
year of work,

72
00:02:25,877 --> 00:02:27,000
before taxes,

73
00:02:27,000 --> 00:02:27,894
before food,

74
00:02:27,894 --> 00:02:29,869
before anything else.

75
00:02:30,117 --> 00:02:33,367
But almost nobody pays for a house

76
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:34,577
with cash.

77
00:02:34,577 --> 00:02:36,007
They borrow.

78
00:02:36,607 --> 00:02:39,486
And the price of borrowing changed even

79
00:02:39,486 --> 00:02:42,179
faster than the price of the house.

80
00:02:52,817 --> 00:02:55,526
In the first week of twenty twenty-one,

81
00:02:55,526 --> 00:02:58,263
the average thirty-year fixed mortgage

82
00:02:58,263 --> 00:02:58,783
rate,

83
00:02:58,783 --> 00:03:00,398
tracked by Freddie Mac,

84
00:03:00,398 --> 00:03:03,187
fell to two point six five percent.

85
00:03:03,277 --> 00:03:05,117
That was the lowest point of that

86
00:03:05,117 --> 00:03:06,177
period.

87
00:03:06,297 --> 00:03:07,802
Then inflation arrived,

88
00:03:07,802 --> 00:03:09,927
and interest rates climbed.

89
00:03:10,087 --> 00:03:12,895
In early November twenty twenty-six,

90
00:03:12,895 --> 00:03:15,254
the same average stood at seven point

91
00:03:15,254 --> 00:03:16,798
one eight percent.

92
00:03:17,050 --> 00:03:19,870
Higher prices times higher rates.

93
00:03:19,870 --> 00:03:22,211
According to Harvard's housing center,

94
00:03:22,211 --> 00:03:24,144
the monthly mortgage payment on the

95
00:03:24,144 --> 00:03:26,292
median-priced home reached two thousand

96
00:03:26,292 --> 00:03:28,610
four hundred and twenty dollars.

97
00:03:28,780 --> 00:03:30,462
At the end of twenty twenty,

98
00:03:30,462 --> 00:03:32,164
it was one thousand two hundred and

99
00:03:32,164 --> 00:03:33,520
forty dollars.

100
00:03:33,610 --> 00:03:35,780
The payment roughly doubled.

101
00:03:35,930 --> 00:03:38,207
Add insurance and property taxes,

102
00:03:38,207 --> 00:03:40,295
and the total monthly cost of owning

103
00:03:40,295 --> 00:03:42,384
that home comes to about three thousand

104
00:03:42,384 --> 00:03:44,655
one hundred and twenty dollars.

105
00:03:44,917 --> 00:03:48,537
And high rates did something strange.

106
00:03:48,537 --> 00:03:50,427
They froze the market.

107
00:03:50,667 --> 00:03:53,079
Half of all outstanding mortgages carried

108
00:03:53,079 --> 00:03:55,271
rates below four percent at the end

109
00:03:55,271 --> 00:03:57,187
of twenty twenty-five.

110
00:03:57,477 --> 00:03:58,386
If you sell,

111
00:03:58,386 --> 00:04:00,019
you give up that cheap loan and

112
00:04:00,019 --> 00:04:02,717
take a new one at around seven.

113
00:04:02,957 --> 00:04:06,027
So many owners simply stay put.

114
00:04:06,283 --> 00:04:08,123
The numbers show it.

115
00:04:08,143 --> 00:04:10,656
The share of Americans who moved in

116
00:04:10,656 --> 00:04:12,484
a year fell to a record low

117
00:04:12,484 --> 00:04:14,998
of eleven point two percent in twenty

118
00:04:14,998 --> 00:04:16,283
twenty-four.

119
00:04:16,583 --> 00:04:19,055
And existing home sales were stuck at

120
00:04:19,055 --> 00:04:21,032
four point one million in twenty

121
00:04:21,032 --> 00:04:21,996
twenty-five,

122
00:04:21,996 --> 00:04:23,207
a thirty-year low,

123
00:04:23,207 --> 00:04:25,432
about two thirds of the sales in

124
00:04:25,432 --> 00:04:27,020
twenty twenty-one.

125
00:04:27,283 --> 00:04:29,263
Let me draw the lock.

126
00:04:29,263 --> 00:04:31,063
Here is an owner with a loan

127
00:04:31,063 --> 00:04:32,563
at three percent.

128
00:04:32,813 --> 00:04:34,276
Here is the house they would like

129
00:04:34,276 --> 00:04:35,503
to move to.

130
00:04:35,653 --> 00:04:36,672
Between them,

131
00:04:36,672 --> 00:04:39,363
a new loan at seven percent.

132
00:04:39,603 --> 00:04:41,890
Moving would mean a much bigger payment

133
00:04:41,890 --> 00:04:43,863
for the same kind of home.

134
00:04:44,093 --> 00:04:45,647
So the owner stays,

135
00:04:45,647 --> 00:04:48,519
the house never comes up for sale,

136
00:04:48,519 --> 00:04:51,154
and the buyers waiting outside see fewer

137
00:04:51,154 --> 00:04:53,335
homes on the market.

138
00:04:53,583 --> 00:04:56,933
Rates explain the last few years.

139
00:04:57,143 --> 00:04:59,191
But the deeper problem started long

140
00:04:59,191 --> 00:04:59,851
before,

141
00:04:59,851 --> 00:05:03,181
with the homes that America never built.

142
00:05:08,817 --> 00:05:11,897
Go back to two thousand and eight.

143
00:05:11,897 --> 00:05:13,626
The housing bubble burst,

144
00:05:13,626 --> 00:05:14,853
builders went bust,

145
00:05:14,853 --> 00:05:17,207
and construction collapsed.

146
00:05:17,317 --> 00:05:20,000
By our calculation from Census data,

147
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:22,481
about about fifteen and a half million

148
00:05:22,481 --> 00:05:24,736
new homes were completed in the two

149
00:05:24,736 --> 00:05:25,787
thousands.

150
00:05:25,867 --> 00:05:27,129
In the twenty tens,

151
00:05:27,129 --> 00:05:29,227
it was about nine million.

152
00:05:29,227 --> 00:05:31,348
In twenty eleven alone,

153
00:05:31,348 --> 00:05:33,970
completions fell to about five hundred and

154
00:05:33,970 --> 00:05:35,377
eighty-five thousand,

155
00:05:35,377 --> 00:05:37,975
the lowest year in the Census records,

156
00:05:37,975 --> 00:05:40,720
which go back to nineteen sixty-eight.

157
00:05:40,983 --> 00:05:41,850
Meanwhile,

158
00:05:41,850 --> 00:05:44,543
the number of households kept growing.

159
00:05:44,543 --> 00:05:46,643
Millennials were reaching the age when

160
00:05:46,643 --> 00:05:48,623
people buy their first home.

161
00:05:48,623 --> 00:05:49,789
Demand rose,

162
00:05:49,789 --> 00:05:52,482
and supply did not keep up.

163
00:05:52,717 --> 00:05:55,117
How many homes are missing?

164
00:05:55,307 --> 00:05:57,757
Nobody agrees on one number.

165
00:05:58,037 --> 00:06:00,203
Harvard's housing center notes that

166
00:06:00,203 --> 00:06:02,850
estimates of the shortage range between

167
00:06:02,850 --> 00:06:04,534
one point two and five point five

168
00:06:04,534 --> 00:06:06,097
million homes.

169
00:06:06,167 --> 00:06:08,075
The research group Up for Growth puts

170
00:06:08,075 --> 00:06:09,983
the gap at about three point eight

171
00:06:09,983 --> 00:06:11,537
million homes.

172
00:06:11,617 --> 00:06:12,616
Either way,

173
00:06:12,616 --> 00:06:15,177
it is millions of homes that people

174
00:06:15,177 --> 00:06:17,569
need and that do not exist.

175
00:06:17,817 --> 00:06:20,817
Builders have picked up the pace again.

176
00:06:20,917 --> 00:06:23,192
Completions came back to about one and

177
00:06:23,192 --> 00:06:26,067
a half million in twenty twenty-five.

178
00:06:26,357 --> 00:06:28,756
But filling a hole that took a

179
00:06:28,756 --> 00:06:31,421
decade to dig takes much longer than

180
00:06:31,421 --> 00:06:32,821
one good year.

181
00:06:33,083 --> 00:06:35,153
Let me draw the gap.

182
00:06:35,203 --> 00:06:37,393
Here is the line of new households,

183
00:06:37,393 --> 00:06:39,983
rising steadily year after year.

184
00:06:40,363 --> 00:06:42,383
Here is the line of new homes.

185
00:06:42,473 --> 00:06:43,461
Before the crash,

186
00:06:43,461 --> 00:06:45,473
the two moved together.

187
00:06:45,663 --> 00:06:46,831
After the crash,

188
00:06:46,831 --> 00:06:48,475
the homes line dropped,

189
00:06:48,475 --> 00:06:50,620
and the space between the two lines

190
00:06:50,620 --> 00:06:52,173
is the shortage.

191
00:06:52,253 --> 00:06:55,045
Too many buyers chasing too few homes

192
00:06:55,045 --> 00:06:56,483
means one thing.

193
00:06:56,553 --> 00:06:58,271
Higher prices.

194
00:06:58,517 --> 00:07:01,527
Which raises an obvious question.

195
00:07:01,717 --> 00:07:03,704
If homes are so valuable,

196
00:07:03,704 --> 00:07:05,867
why not just build more of them?

197
00:07:06,087 --> 00:07:06,653
Often,

198
00:07:06,653 --> 00:07:09,205
the answer is written in the rules.

199
00:07:14,850 --> 00:07:16,833
In most American cities,

200
00:07:16,833 --> 00:07:19,283
local zoning rules decide what can be

201
00:07:19,283 --> 00:07:21,220
built on each piece of land.

202
00:07:21,530 --> 00:07:22,930
And very often,

203
00:07:22,930 --> 00:07:25,041
the answer is one house per lot,

204
00:07:25,041 --> 00:07:26,590
and nothing else.

205
00:07:27,070 --> 00:07:28,874
Researchers at the University of

206
00:07:28,874 --> 00:07:29,678
California,

207
00:07:29,678 --> 00:07:30,318
Berkeley,

208
00:07:30,318 --> 00:07:32,230
mapped Greater Los Angeles.

209
00:07:32,420 --> 00:07:34,038
They found that about seventy-eight

210
00:07:34,038 --> 00:07:36,060
percent of its residential land is

211
00:07:36,060 --> 00:07:39,289
reserved for single-family homes only.

212
00:07:39,550 --> 00:07:41,341
That means no apartments,

213
00:07:41,341 --> 00:07:42,612
no townhouses,

214
00:07:42,612 --> 00:07:43,884
no small duplex,

215
00:07:43,884 --> 00:07:45,960
on most of the land where people

216
00:07:45,960 --> 00:07:46,820
live.

217
00:07:47,130 --> 00:07:49,146
Even where demand is highest,

218
00:07:49,146 --> 00:07:51,184
the rules can keep the number of

219
00:07:51,184 --> 00:07:52,690
homes fixed.

220
00:07:52,950 --> 00:07:54,758
Then there is the cost of the

221
00:07:54,758 --> 00:07:56,650
rules themselves.

222
00:07:56,730 --> 00:07:57,153
Permits,

223
00:07:57,153 --> 00:07:57,430
fees,

224
00:07:57,430 --> 00:07:58,520
codes and delays.

225
00:07:58,520 --> 00:08:01,789
The National Association of Home Builders,

226
00:08:01,789 --> 00:08:03,538
the builders' own trade group,

227
00:08:03,538 --> 00:08:07,085
estimates that regulation makes up about

228
00:08:07,085 --> 00:08:09,619
twenty-six point four percent of the price

229
00:08:09,619 --> 00:08:12,500
of a new single-family home.

230
00:08:12,550 --> 00:08:14,170
On an average new home,

231
00:08:14,170 --> 00:08:16,425
that is about one hundred and thirty-two

232
00:08:16,425 --> 00:08:17,846
thousand dollars.

233
00:08:18,083 --> 00:08:20,548
That estimate comes from the industry,

234
00:08:20,548 --> 00:08:23,034
and many rules protect safety and the

235
00:08:23,034 --> 00:08:24,463
environment.

236
00:08:24,603 --> 00:08:26,653
But the direction is clear.

237
00:08:26,653 --> 00:08:29,312
Every extra step makes a new home

238
00:08:29,312 --> 00:08:32,087
slower and more expensive to build.

239
00:08:32,350 --> 00:08:34,630
Let me draw one city block.

240
00:08:34,630 --> 00:08:36,260
Here are eight lots.

241
00:08:36,260 --> 00:08:38,213
With single-family zoning,

242
00:08:38,213 --> 00:08:39,730
each lot gets one house.

243
00:08:39,730 --> 00:08:40,960
Eight homes.

244
00:08:41,000 --> 00:08:43,310
Now allow small apartment buildings on the

245
00:08:43,310 --> 00:08:44,330
same lots.

246
00:08:44,360 --> 00:08:46,030
Four homes on each.

247
00:08:46,030 --> 00:08:48,416
Thirty-two homes on the same block,

248
00:08:48,416 --> 00:08:49,980
with the same streets.

249
00:08:50,120 --> 00:08:51,850
The land did not change.

250
00:08:51,850 --> 00:08:53,574
Only the rule did.

251
00:08:53,817 --> 00:08:55,015
High prices,

252
00:08:55,015 --> 00:08:56,520
expensive loans,

253
00:08:56,520 --> 00:08:59,577
missing homes and tight rules.

254
00:08:59,617 --> 00:09:01,048
Put them together,

255
00:09:01,048 --> 00:09:03,832
and someone ends up locked outside.

256
00:09:09,483 --> 00:09:12,343
The National Association of Realtors

257
00:09:12,343 --> 00:09:14,243
tracks who buys homes.

258
00:09:14,253 --> 00:09:15,877
In its latest survey,

259
00:09:15,877 --> 00:09:18,229
the typical first-time buyer was forty

260
00:09:18,229 --> 00:09:18,912
years old,

261
00:09:18,912 --> 00:09:20,453
an all-time high.

262
00:09:20,643 --> 00:09:22,768
First-time buyers made up just twenty-two

263
00:09:22,768 --> 00:09:24,160
percent of all buyers,

264
00:09:24,160 --> 00:09:26,993
about half the historical norm before two

265
00:09:26,993 --> 00:09:28,538
thousand and eight.

266
00:09:28,783 --> 00:09:30,991
And there is little on the shelf

267
00:09:30,991 --> 00:09:32,143
for them.

268
00:09:32,563 --> 00:09:35,179
According to Harvard's housing center,

269
00:09:35,179 --> 00:09:37,074
in March twenty nineteen,

270
00:09:37,074 --> 00:09:39,714
forty-nine percent of homes listed for

271
00:09:39,714 --> 00:09:42,593
sale were affordable to a household

272
00:09:42,593 --> 00:09:45,233
earning seventy-five thousand dollars or

273
00:09:45,233 --> 00:09:46,073
less.

274
00:09:46,383 --> 00:09:48,616
By March twenty twenty-six,

275
00:09:48,616 --> 00:09:51,477
it was just twenty-three percent.

276
00:09:51,717 --> 00:09:54,744
The squeeze reaches far beyond the big

277
00:09:54,744 --> 00:09:56,857
coastal cities.

278
00:09:57,137 --> 00:09:58,575
In twenty twenty,

279
00:09:58,575 --> 00:10:01,011
you needed more than one hundred thousand

280
00:10:01,011 --> 00:10:03,448
dollars a year to afford the median

281
00:10:03,448 --> 00:10:05,997
home in thirty-one metro areas.

282
00:10:06,217 --> 00:10:07,752
In twenty twenty-five,

283
00:10:07,752 --> 00:10:10,355
it was one hundred and sixty-nine.

284
00:10:10,617 --> 00:10:13,737
Let me put the four forces together.

285
00:10:13,757 --> 00:10:14,612
The price,

286
00:10:14,612 --> 00:10:17,247
almost five years of income.

287
00:10:17,247 --> 00:10:17,976
The loan,

288
00:10:17,976 --> 00:10:20,337
at around seven percent.

289
00:10:20,377 --> 00:10:21,801
The missing homes,

290
00:10:21,801 --> 00:10:23,547
counted in millions.

291
00:10:23,547 --> 00:10:25,597
And the rules on the land.

292
00:10:25,707 --> 00:10:28,322
